.TH LMFS 4
.SH NAME
lmfs, aux/llama \- a language model served as files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lmfs
[
.B -Dc
] [
.B -b
.I bytes
] [
.B -e
.I engine
] [
.B -l
.I dir
] [
.B -m
.I mtpt
] [
.B -s
.I srvname
] [
.I model
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Lmfs
serves a language model's files on
.I mtpt
.RB ( /mnt/lm
by default, or none if only
.B -s
is given), and posts itself as
.BI /srv/ srvname
if
.B -s
is given.
Its files and their answers are those of
.IR lmsim (4),
which describes them:
.BR models ,
.BR ctl ,
.BR clone ,
and each session's
.BR ctl ,
.BR data ,
.B top
and
.BR embed .
A model is a GGUF file in
.I dir
.RB ( /lib/lm ),
named for the file without
.BR .gguf ;
a model's package adds its file there.
A session starts with
.IR model ,
or the first of
.BR models .
.PP
The model runs in
.BR aux/llama ,
.IR engine 's
default, a program of llama.cpp's on ggml's WebGPU backend on
.IR gpu (3)
where the browser has a GPU, and else, or with
.BR -c ,
on ggml's CPU backend, in one thread.
On the GPU it works out each token's bits and rank there, and reads back those alone.
.I Lmfs
starts one for a model's tokenizer and one for the model, and talks to each on a pipe,
a request a line, so the model's work runs beside the server and never holds up
another session's open, read or
.BR ctl .
It loads a model once, for every session that names it,
and at its start only a
.I model
named on its command line.
.I Bytes
.RB ( -b ,
3 GB by default, of wasm32's 4)
is one bound for every engine's model, caches and scratch, as the engine measures its heap:
a model that would pass it fails to load, and its sessions' reads fail with the reason, which
.B ctl
gives as
.BR error .
An engine keeps as many sequences as fit, and fails if none does.
.I Lmfs
holds 64 MB of requests' text at most, and a write past that fails, and keeps 1 MB of a
request's answer unread before its work waits.
An engine that fails to load is not started again for 2 seconds, then 4, up to a minute.
Each
.B ctx
and
.B kv
has an engine of its own, and a model's engine for another ends when its work does.
.PP
.I Lmfs
cuts a text into parts as
.IR lmsim (4)
says, and scores each with the
.B ctx
tokens before it, and with the model's start-of-text token before the text's first.
It keeps each part's bits and ranks by the hash of its tokens, those before it,
the model's SHA-256, the backend,
.B kv
and
.BR ctx ,
for 8 million tokens.
The engine keeps the model's state for the parts it scored last: a transformer's keys and
values, or a recurrent model's state every 256 tokens, so an edit inside a part scores again
only from the edit.
It decodes in blocks at fixed places, of 64 tokens on the CPU and 256 on the GPU,
so a part's bits do not depend on what it decoded before, and
.B data
gives a part's lines a block at a time, as the model scores them.
The GPU's bits differ from the CPU's by about 0.1 of a bit a token, on average.
A byte that is not UTF-8 is its byte's token.
.B Embed
gives the model's last hidden state, the mean over the span's tokens, of unit length.
.PP
.B Freq
gives each token's slot by
.IR lmsim (4)'s
rule, with each token's probability from a softmax, in double precision, of the model's
logits.
The engine runs a block a token at a time, from the start-of-text token, when it encodes as
when it decodes, so the two add the same sums in the same order, and on the CPU in wasm those
sums are the same on every machine.
It keeps each block of a set in a sequence of its own, and runs a block that has lost its
sequence to another again from its start.
A block is at most
.B ctx
+ 4096 tokens.
The engine takes
.BR freq 's
work a few tokens at a time, and the other files' requests go first.
.PP
.B Ctl
takes
.BR model ,
.B backend
.RB ( float ;
.B exact
is not built) and
.B ctx
(up to
.BR maxctx ),
and
.BI digits " n\fR,"
the decimal places of
.BR data 's
and
.BR top 's
bits, 2 by default and 17 at most.
.BR Settings ,
for
.IR lmz 's
header, names the model, its SHA-256, the backend and the engine's pinned settings, not
.BR ctx .
.B Device
says where the model runs,
.B gpu
or
.BR cpu .
A read gives the
.B sha256
as
.B loading
until the engine has loaded the model.
.B Maxctx
is the smaller of the context the model was trained on (40,960 tokens for Qwen3-0.6B) and the
longest whose keys and values, with a part's, fit the device beside the model and its scratch:
#γ's session of 2 GiB on the GPU, and
.I bytes
on the CPU.
A read gives it once an engine of the model at the server's
.B kv
has loaded, at any
.BR ctx ,
and else gives the word it gives for
.BR sha256 ;
until then a
.B ctx
past it fails at the engine's load.
.PP
The server's
.B ctl
takes
.BR "kv f16" ,
the default, or
.BR "kv q8_0" ,
for the engines started after it: q8_0 keeps the model's keys in 8 bits and its values in f16,
so a device holds a longer
.BR ctx :
Qwen3-0.6B on the GPU holds 8192 at f16 and 11,776 at q8_0, and on the CPU 12,288 at f16.
Its tokens on the GPU at q8_0 differ from the CPU's at f16 by about 0.17 of a bit on
average, where f16's differ by 0.12.
A request takes the
.B kv
of the moment it is opened.
.PP
The packages are downloads: Granite-4.0-H-350M is 366 MB and Qwen3-0.6B 639 MB.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
% pkg add qwen3-0.6b
% lmfs
% echo the cat sat on the mat. | bits
.EE
.PP
Keep keys in 8 bits from the next engine on, and see the longest
.B ctx
then:
.EX
% echo kv q8_0 >/mnt/lm/ctl
% echo the cat sat on the mat. | bits >/dev/null
% <>/mnt/lm/clone { grep maxctx /mnt/lm/`{read}^/ctl }
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B src/cmd/lmfs
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR lmsim (4),
.IR bits (1),
.IR pkg (1),
.B doc/words.md
.SH BUGS
A set of more blocks than the engine has sequences runs each block again from its start at
every step, and takes time in proportion to the square of its length.
The engine scores one part at a time, not parts in batches.
On the CPU in wasm, a model of half a billion weights scores 5 to 15 tokens a second.
On the GPU, ggml waits 30 seconds at most for the GPU to finish a block,
and else ends the engine;
the model's requests then fail with ggml's message until
.I lmfs
is started again.
